  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A block of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math>&nbsp;slides on a frictionless surface with an initial speed of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>. On top of the block, there is a small block&nbsp;of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>.&nbsp;The coefficient of friction between the blocks&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>μ</mi></math>. The system of blocks encounters an ideal spring with force constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi></math>. The maximum value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi></math>&nbsp;for which the blocks don't slip relative to each other is</p>\r\r<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0040_6a2a116228c5.jpg\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 84px;\" /></p>\r"
  },
